@provenance Corneli 2016, "An institutional approach to computational social creativity", ICCC 2016 (https://metameso.org/~joe/papers/corneli2016institutional.pdf), Table 2 ("creativity design principles") and the sections that elaborate it. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@summary Joe's 2016 transposition of Ostrom's design principles from
managing a common-pool resource to contributing in a creativity
commons. The polarity is switched from appropriation to contribution,
Rhodes's 4Ps become Producer, Process, Product and Place, and each
principle is tied to a computational test. Because rules are tests, the
institutions themselves become Products of the commons. One pattern per
principle (eleven items), plus institutions-are-products for that
recursive reading. Ostrom's originals are the parallel family
institutions/.

! conclusion:
  Ostrom's theory of institutions can be used to design systems that
  exhibit computational social creativity, provided the principles are
  restated for contribution rather than appropriation and expressed as
  tests that Producers can run, read and change, including tests on the
  institutions themselves.

  + context:
    Ostrom's commons are rivalrous and nonexcludable. A creative commons
    such as Wikipedia, an "expressive commons" (Safner 2016), is
    non-rivalrous in consumption; its social dilemmas are in
    contribution (hence policies like Neutral Point Of View). The 2016
    paper therefore rewrites Table 1 "replacing the focus on
    appropriation with a focus on contribution". It renders Rhodes's 4Ps
    as Producer, Process, Product and Place, labels that are more
    inclusive and abstract than Rhodes's, so that Producers need not be
    persons. The Place is Ostrom's action situation, structured in
    advance by context. Software testing (assertions, advice, contracts,
    test-driven development) supplies the technical form: each principle
    becomes something a test can check.

  + IF:
    Humans and machines produce together in a shared Place, and the
    rules of that Place are being written.

  + HOWEVER:
    The principles were offered as "guidelines (and with minor changes,
    hypotheses)" that need empirical work. Some are thin when the
    Producer is a machine: 2B (benefit for effort) is "perfectly
    meaningless" for non-agentive machines, and 7 (rights to organise)
    meets the programmer as the main external authority. In 2016 agents
    with the assumed abilities did "not exist yet in any robust form";
    now they do, so the patterns can be tried rather than only reasoned
    about.

  + THEN:
    Design or review a Place with the twelve patterns. Where Table 2
    departs from Ostrom, follow Table 2: sanctions come from other
    Producers and range from critique to silence (5); monitoring becomes
    tests that document Producer-Place interaction (4A) and tests that
    Producers can modify (4B), not monitors of users and of a resource.
    Treat the institution as one of the Products (institutions-are-
    products). Evaluate with the correspondence the paper draws between
    IAD and SPECS: context to definition, action to criteria, outcome to
    evaluation.

  + BECAUSE:
    Rules are if-then statements, so changing the rules means writing
    new tests; and "our institutions – including institutions for
    building institutions – are analysable in programmatic terms". A
    commons whose rules are tests its members can change can design its
    own institutions, which is what makes the approach recursive.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Correct the drafts from runs, not by reading: apply the family to the FUTON Emacs fork (maintainer rulings, reporters as testers), to Agency dispatch rules, and to a mathematics workflow.]
    next[Mine closed debbugs threads for how maintainers settled design questions: local-condition material (2A) for a separate emacs/ family.]
    next[Review Alinery's playbook guidance and our FIX-RULE.md side by side against principles 3 and 4B.]
